I've looked at nethack sources.  My career has been 
mostly in software QA, so I couldn't stop myself from
looking.  It's a classic example of unplanned feature 
creep overwhelming a simple program architecture.  It 
looks like it may have begun as a well-organized and 
reasonably well-planned program, but every deviation 
from some very simple behavior (and nethack has a lot 
of such deviations by now) is handled as a special case.  
By the time all the special cases are checked for and 
handled, the original program architecture is very 
obscured.  Furthermore, since all the checks for special 
cases have to happen along the same control path, 
independently developed patches can interfere with one 
another very easily. 

I started out with the idea that there would be a lot 
of special cases, so I designed an architecture where 
there's a standard way to integrate the checks and the 
execution of the associated special behavior, and 
handling them is a normal and fundamental part of all 
event processing.  

The two most fundamental concepts are "actors" and 
"events."  An "event" is something that happens which 
changes the state of the game - something takes damage, 
something is opened, something is broken, etc.  Events 
involve up to six actors; a subject (who's doing the 
action), an object (who or which it's being done to), an 
agent (weapon or tool that's being used to do it), an 
agency (conveyor of effect, secondary tool, or ammo), 
an instigator (the creature or item "responsible" for the 
action, for purposes of experience awarded, conducts, 
alignment, or anger/fear responses I haven't coded yet) 
and a timing reference (an actor which, if sped, slowed, 
paralyzed, or destroyed, will accordingly change the 
timing of the event or cancel it).  Usually the instigator,
subject, and timing reference are the same actor, but 
they don't have to be.

Each actor has a resizable hash table to hold its special 
properties, which includes all kinds of things, but most 
interestingly "triggers."  A trigger is hashed by a 
combination of event and role within that event, designated 
"before" or "after", and contains several parameters plus 
a pointer to an executable method.  During normal event 
processing, the event and all actors' roles in the event 
are known, and the system checks for triggers on all the 
actors involved, jumping to and executing their triggered 
methods if they have any.  The methods can modify the impending 
event if they're "before" methods.  They can also schedule 
new events, queue messages to the user, or do some other 
things. But they're not allowed to directly modify any 
actors; only events should do that.

As an example, if we have a FrostBrand-type sword, It'll 
have a trigger keyed something like TR_BEFORE_MWATTACK_AGENT
(meaning, something to do before being used as an agent by 
an EV_MWATTACK event) storing some parameters and a code pointer.  
When we get the next event from the schedule, and it's an 
EV_MWATTACK (meelee attack with a weapon) we check the actors 
involved for TR_BEFORE_<event>_<role> triggers corresponding
to their role and that event.  We find that the agent (weapon) 
has a TR_BEFORE_MWATTACK_AGENT trigger, so we jump to its 
method using the pointer stored under that trigger.  The 
routine gets pointers to the EV_MWATTACK event record and the
TR_BEFORE_MWATTACK_AGENT hash table record, so it has access 
to everything in both records as parameters, notably including 
the identities of other actors in the event. It can modify 
the event adding extra cold damage before the event actually 
happens.  Then we jump to the code pointer in the EV_MWATTACK 
event, which executes the (modified) event itself (it gets a 
pointer to the event record as its parameter so all the 
bundled arguments are passed to it).  Then we check for and 
execute "after" triggers, and we're done with one event.
We get the next event from the schedule, rinse, repeat 
until game over. 

The idea is that this general property check, which is 
done in one place by the event processing code, provides 
the attachment point for any and all special behavior that 
is reasonable to want to attach to any particular actor.  
And yes, an actor can have multiple triggers on the same 
role and the same event. If so they all get called.

To add a special case, you select a trigger method, or 
write one from scratch, then just insert a property on 
the specialized actor pointing to that code and keyed by 
the event and the actor's role in that event. You don't 
wind up interfering with other special-behavior patches 
because you don't even touch the main execution sequence. 
The program finds the trigger when it's doing normal event 
processing and jumps to your method, either before or 
after the event is processed as you specify.  You can 
also add event types, and if you do the makefile will 
pick them up and create corresponding triggers for them, 
so you don't have to worry about keeping all of this 
machinery synchronized by hand. 

Some events are "virtual."  The actual event code in this 
case does nothing, but processing the event causes a check 
for and execution of triggered specializations that do all 
the work.  

Here I've been talking about "Code pointers" but the actual 
implementation uses integer indices into vectors of code 
pointers, both for triggered methods and event code.  The 
declarations of these vectors of code pointers are once 
again autogenerated by the makefile; you define the routine, 
the build process will put pointers to it into the tables.  
This allows both the schedule (containing all events that 
haven't happened yet) and the actors (with their property 
tables) to be reliably serialized.

And yes, I realize the irony of rolling my own OO system 
using code pointers, method tables, closure records, and 
autogenerated code in C. The problem was that C++/Java/et 
al didn't have the *right* kind of OO for this situation.  
I could have done it more easily in Common Lisp using CLOS 
and the MetaObject Protocol.   But then I'd have been 
writing a game just for people who already have a Common 
Lisp runtime installed.  I could probably have added kluges 
to one of the so-called "OO languages" to make its OO work 
the way I needed.  But it would have been ugly and even 
more arcane than what I did.  

Besides, I like C.  I like the old-fashioned hardcore-ness 
of it. 

